DSP.mod(10,{"m10-dl-fundamentals":{"id":"m10-dl-fundamentals","slug":"deep-learning-fundamentals","m":10,"title":"Deep learning fundamentals","min":45,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":["m7-optimization"],"tags":["deep-learning","neural-networks"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Neural network fundamentals: neurons and layers, activations and losses, backpropagation, optimisers, training problems and fixes, major architectures and a PyTorch training loop read line by line.","keyTerms":["neuron","activation functions","backpropagation","loss","Adam","dropout","batch norm","vanishing gradients","CNN","RNN","LSTM","autoencoder","transfer learning","PyTorch"],"interviewQs":["Explain backpropagation in simple terms.","Why ReLU instead of sigmoid in hidden layers?","How do you reduce overfitting in a neural network?","Batch norm vs layer norm?"],"mistakes":["Forgetting model.train() and model.eval()","Using softmax before CrossEntropyLoss in PyTorch","Training without normalised inputs"],"practice":"Train a small MLP on a tabular dataset, plot train and validation loss, and apply early stopping and dropout.","related":["m7-optimization","m10-transformers","m10-nlp"],"points":"- Neuron: output = activation(w.x + b). MLP: stacked layers.\n- Activations: sigmoid (binary output, vanishing gradient), tanh, ReLU (default hidden), Leaky ReLU, GELU, softmax (multi-class output).\n- Loss: MSE (regression), binary cross-entropy, categorical cross-entropy.\n- Backpropagation: chain rule to compute gradients layer by layer; then optimizer updates weights.\n- Optimizers: SGD (+momentum), RMSprop, Adam/AdamW.\n- Training issues and fixes:\n\nProblem - Fix\nOverfitting - Dropout, weight decay, early stopping, data augmentation, smaller model, more data\nVanishing/exploding gradients - ReLU-family, proper init (He/Xavier), batch/layer norm, residual connections, gradient clipping\nSlow/unstable training - Tune learning rate, scheduler, normalize inputs, batch size\nClass imbalance - Weighted loss, focal loss, resampling\n\n- Batch norm vs layer norm: normalize across batch vs across features (layer norm used in transformers).\n- Architectures (conceptual):\n- CNN: convolutions, pooling; spatial hierarchies; images.\n- RNN/LSTM/GRU: sequences; gating handles long dependencies.\n- Autoencoders: compression/denoising/anomaly detection.\n- Transformers: self-attention, parallelizable, dominate NLP/vision now.\n- Transfer learning: reuse pre-trained model; freeze early layers, fine-tune later ones.\n- Frameworks: PyTorch (preferred in industry/research), TensorFlow/Keras."},"intuitive":"<p>A neural network is a stack of simple &quot;voting&quot; units. Each unit adds up its inputs with weights (like scoring a job applicant on several criteria), then decides how strongly to fire. Stack many layers and the network builds understanding step by step: edges, then shapes, then faces. Training is trial and error at scale: show an example, see how wrong the answer was, and nudge every weight slightly in the direction that would have made it less wrong. Working out which weights to nudge and by how much, layer by layer backwards, is backpropagation.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Even if you mostly use trees at work, interviews check you understand the basics of how neural nets learn.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Weighted sums plus non-linearities, trained by gradient descent with gradients from backprop.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A neural network is layers of simple units. Each unit takes a weighted sum of its inputs plus a bias and passes it through an activation function like ReLU, which adds the non-linearity that lets the network learn curved patterns. The forward pass computes predictions; a loss measures the error; backpropagation uses the chain rule to get the gradient of the loss for every weight; an optimiser such as Adam updates them. Overfitting is controlled with dropout, weight decay, early stopping and more data. Vanishing gradients, where signals shrink through many layers, are eased by ReLU, good initialisation, batch normalisation and residual connections. The catch: for small tabular data, gradient boosting usually beats deep learning.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"One neuron learns, step by step","html":"<p>Inputs x = [1, 2], weights w = [0.5, −0.25], bias b = 0, sigmoid output. The true label is 1. Learning rate 0.5.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Forward pass:</strong> z = 0.5 × 1 + (−0.25) × 2 + 0 = <strong>0</strong>. p = sigmoid(0) = <strong>0.5</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Loss</strong> (log loss for label 1): −ln(0.5) ≈ <strong>0.693</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Backward pass.</strong> For sigmoid with log loss, the error signal at z is simply p − y = 0.5 − 1 = <strong>−0.5</strong>. Each weight&#39;s gradient is that signal times its input: [−0.5 × 1, −0.5 × 2] = [−0.5, −1.0]. The bias gradient is −0.5.</li>\n<li><strong>Update:</strong> w ← w − 0.5 × gradient = [0.5 + 0.25, −0.25 + 0.5] = <strong>[0.75, 0.25]</strong>; b ← 0 + 0.25 = <strong>0.25</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Forward again:</strong> z = 0.75 + 0.5 + 0.25 = 1.5, p ≈ <strong>0.82</strong>, loss ≈ <strong>0.20</strong>. One step moved the prediction from 0.5 towards the true label.</li>\n<li><strong>A network repeats this</strong> for millions of weights. Backpropagation is the chain rule passing the error signal backwards through each layer, so every weight learns its share of the blame.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Training is forward pass, loss, gradient, small step, repeated. The input with the larger value (x₂ = 2) received the larger correction.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-building-blocks","text":"Building blocks","level":3},{"id":"h-backpropagation-in-plain-terms","text":"Backpropagation, in plain terms","level":3},{"id":"h-training-problems-and-fixes","text":"Training problems and fixes","level":3},{"id":"h-architectures-conceptual","text":"Architectures (conceptual)","level":3},{"id":"h-a-pytorch-loop-read-line-by-line","text":"A PyTorch loop, read line by line","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Neuron:</strong> output = activation(w.x + b). <strong>MLP:</strong> stacked layers.</li>\n<li><strong>Activations:</strong> sigmoid (binary output, vanishing gradient), tanh, <strong>ReLU</strong> (default hidden), Leaky ReLU, GELU, <strong>softmax</strong> (multi-class output).</li>\n<li><strong>Loss:</strong> MSE (regression), binary cross-entropy, categorical cross-entropy.</li>\n<li><strong>Backpropagation:</strong> chain rule to compute gradients layer by layer; then optimizer updates weights.</li>\n<li><strong>Optimizers:</strong> SGD (+momentum), RMSprop, <strong>Adam/AdamW</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Training issues and fixes:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Overfitting</td><td>Dropout, weight decay, early stopping, data augmentation, smaller model, more data</td></tr><tr><td>Vanishing/exploding gradients</td><td>ReLU-family, proper init (He/Xavier), batch/layer norm, residual connections, gradient clipping</td></tr><tr><td>Slow/unstable training</td><td>Tune learning rate, scheduler, normalize inputs, batch size</td></tr><tr><td>Class imbalance</td><td>Weighted loss, focal loss, resampling</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Batch norm vs layer norm:</strong> normalize across batch vs across features (layer norm used in transformers).</li>\n<li><strong>Architectures (conceptual):</strong><ul>\n<li><strong>CNN:</strong> convolutions, pooling; spatial hierarchies; images.</li>\n<li><strong>RNN/LSTM/GRU:</strong> sequences; gating handles long dependencies.</li>\n<li><strong>Autoencoders:</strong> compression/denoising/anomaly detection.</li>\n<li><strong>Transformers:</strong> self-attention, parallelizable, dominate NLP/vision now.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Transfer learning:</strong> reuse pre-trained model; freeze early layers, fine-tune later ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Frameworks:</strong> PyTorch (preferred in industry/research), TensorFlow/Keras.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> torch, torch.nn <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> nn\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">MLP</span>(nn.Module):\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, d_in, d_hidden=<span class=\"hljs-number\">64</span></span>):\n        <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().__init__()\n        <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(d_in, d_hidden), nn.ReLU(), nn.Dropout(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.2</span>),\n                                 nn.Linear(d_hidden, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>))\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">forward</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, x</span>): <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.net(x).squeeze(-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\n\nmodel = MLP(d_in=<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>)\nopt = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1e-3</span>)\nloss_fn = nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> epoch <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>):\n    model.train()\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> xb, yb <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> train_loader:\n        opt.zero_grad()\n        loss = loss_fn(model(xb), yb)\n        loss.backward()\n        opt.step()</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-building-blocks\">Building blocks</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neuron:</strong> <code>output = activation(w·x + b)</code>. A <strong>layer</strong> holds many neurons; an <strong>MLP</strong> stacks dense layers.</li>\n<li><strong>Activations</strong> add non-linearity (without them, stacked layers collapse to one linear map):</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Activation</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Sigmoid</strong></td><td>squashes to (0, 1): binary output probability; saturates, so <strong>vanishing gradients</strong> in deep hidden layers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>tanh</strong></td><td>(−1, 1), zero-centred; still saturates</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ReLU</strong> <code>max(0, x)</code></td><td>the default hidden activation: cheap, no saturation for positive inputs; can &quot;die&quot; if stuck at 0</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Leaky ReLU / GELU</strong></td><td>avoid dead units; GELU is common in transformers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Softmax</strong></td><td>turns scores into a probability distribution over classes (output layer)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Losses:</strong> MSE (regression), binary cross-entropy, categorical cross-entropy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-backpropagation-in-plain-terms\">Backpropagation, in plain terms</h3>\n<p>The forward pass computes the prediction and loss. <strong>Backpropagation</strong> applies the <strong>chain rule</strong> to compute the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight, working backwards from the output: each layer&#39;s gradient is its local derivative times the gradient arriving from the layer above. An optimiser (SGD, <strong>Adam/AdamW</strong>) then updates the weights: <code>w ← w − η · gradient</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-training-problems-and-fixes\">Training problems and fixes</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Overfitting</td><td><strong>dropout</strong>, weight decay, early stopping, data augmentation, smaller model, more data</td></tr><tr><td>Vanishing / exploding gradients</td><td>ReLU family, proper init (<strong>He/Xavier</strong>), batch or layer norm, <strong>residual connections</strong>, gradient clipping</td></tr><tr><td>Slow or unstable training</td><td>tune learning rate, add a scheduler, normalise inputs, adjust batch size</td></tr><tr><td>Class imbalance</td><td>weighted loss, <strong>focal loss</strong>, resampling</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Batch norm vs layer norm.</strong> Batch norm normalises each feature across the <strong>batch</strong> (works well in CNNs, depends on batch statistics); <strong>layer norm</strong> normalises across the <strong>features of each sample</strong> (independent of batch size, used in transformers and RNNs).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-architectures-conceptual\">Architectures (conceptual)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CNN:</strong> convolutional filters slide over images, sharing weights, plus pooling; learns spatial hierarchies. Output size for a 32-wide input with a 3×3 kernel, padding 1, stride 1 is <code>(32 − 3 + 2·1)/1 + 1 = 32</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>RNN / LSTM / GRU:</strong> process sequences step by step with a hidden state; <strong>gates</strong> in LSTM/GRU control what to remember and forget, easing long-range dependencies. Largely replaced by transformers for NLP.</li>\n<li><strong>Autoencoders:</strong> compress and reconstruct; used for denoising and anomaly detection.</li>\n<li><strong>Transformers:</strong> self-attention, parallel over the sequence; now dominant in language and increasingly vision (next topics).</li>\n<li><strong>Transfer learning:</strong> start from a pre-trained model, <strong>freeze early layers</strong>, fine-tune later ones on your small dataset.</li>\n<li><strong>Frameworks:</strong> PyTorch (dominant in industry and research), TensorFlow/Keras.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-pytorch-loop-read-line-by-line\">A PyTorch loop, read line by line</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> torch, torch.nn <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> nn\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">class</span> <span class=\"hljs-title class_\">MLP</span>(nn.Module):\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">__init__</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, d_in, d_hidden=<span class=\"hljs-number\">64</span></span>):\n        <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">super</span>().__init__()\n        <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(d_in, d_hidden), nn.ReLU(), nn.Dropout(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.2</span>), nn.Linear(d_hidden, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>))\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">forward</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">self, x</span>): <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-variable language_\">self</span>.net(x).squeeze(-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># raw scores (logits), shape (batch,)</span>\n\nmodel = MLP(d_in=<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>)                                           <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># parameters: 20*64 + 64 + 64*1 + 1 = 1,409</span>\nopt = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1e-3</span>)\nloss_fn = nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()                               <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># applies the sigmoid internally: feed it LOGITS, not probabilities</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> epoch <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>):\n    model.train()                                              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># enables dropout</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> xb, yb <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> train_loader:\n        opt.zero_grad()                                        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># clear the previous gradients</span>\n        loss = loss_fn(model(xb), yb)                          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># forward pass + loss</span>\n        loss.backward()                                        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># backpropagation: fills .grad for every parameter</span>\n        opt.step()                                             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># weight update</span>\n    model.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">eval</span>()                                               <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># disables dropout for validation</span>\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">with</span> torch.no_grad():\n        val_loss = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>(loss_fn(model(xb), yb).item() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> xb, yb <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> val_loader) / <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(val_loader)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Things interviewers check: <code>zero_grad</code> each step (gradients accumulate), <code>train()</code> vs <code>eval()</code>, <code>no_grad</code> during validation, logits with <code>BCEWithLogitsLoss</code> (and raw logits with <code>CrossEntropyLoss</code> for multi-class: no softmax beforehand), and <strong>early stopping</strong> on the validation loss.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m10-dl-fundamentals~1","q":"Why can&#39;t a neural network with only linear activations learn a non-linear function?","a":"<p>If every layer computes <code>Wx + b</code>, then two layers give <code>W₂(W₁x + b₁) + b₂ = (W₂W₁)x + (W₂b₁ + b₂)</code>, which is still linear. Non-linear activations (ReLU, tanh) between layers let the network approximate arbitrary non-linear functions (the universal approximation idea). This is why the choice of activation matters, and why a network without them is just a linear or logistic regression.</p>\n","s":"A composition of linear maps is itself a linear map, so extra layers add nothing without non-linear activations."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-dl-fundamentals~ca0_1","q":"Why do networks need non-linear activations?","a":"<p>Without them, stacked linear layers collapse into a single linear function and cannot model complex patterns.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-dl-fundamentals~ca0_2","q":"What does backpropagation compute?","a":"<p>The gradient of the loss with respect to every weight, efficiently reusing intermediate results via the chain rule.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-dl-fundamentals~ca0_3","q":"Training loss falls, validation loss rises. Three remedies?","a":"<p>Dropout or weight decay, early stopping, and more data or augmentation (or a smaller network).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m10-dl-fundamentals~ta1_1","q":"<p>A two-layer network uses no activation function: hidden = 2x + 1, output = 3 × hidden − 2. Simplify the output as a function of x. What does this show about why activations are needed?</p>\n","a":"<p>Output = 3(2x + 1) − 2 = 6x + 1, which is just another straight line. Stacking linear layers always collapses to a single linear function, however many layers you add. A non-linear activation such as ReLU, max(0, z), between the layers is what lets a network bend and represent curved patterns.</p>\n"}]},"m10-nlp":{"id":"m10-nlp","slug":"nlp-classical-pipeline-tf-idf-and-embeddings","m":10,"title":"NLP: classical pipeline, TF-IDF and embeddings","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":[],"tags":["nlp","text"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The classical NLP pipeline, text representations from bag of words to TF-IDF to embeddings, a worked TF-IDF calculation, common tasks and the Indian-language context.","keyTerms":["tokenization","stemming","lemmatization","bag of words","TF-IDF","n-grams","word embeddings","sentence embeddings","code-mixed text","Hinglish"],"interviewQs":["Explain TF-IDF and when it beats embeddings.","Stemming vs lemmatisation?","How would you handle Hinglish text?"],"mistakes":["Removing stopwords when they matter (for example in sentiment with negation)","Fitting the vectoriser on all data before splitting","Using a heavy model without a TF-IDF baseline"],"practice":"Build a TF-IDF + logistic regression sentiment classifier and compare it with a sentence-embedding version.","related":["m10-transformers","m7-naive-bayes","m14-portfolio"],"points":"Classical pipeline: lowercase -> tokenize -> remove stopwords/punctuation -> stem (crude chop) or lemmatize (dictionary-based) -> vectorize.\n\nRepresentation - Notes\nBag of Words / CountVectorizer - Word counts; sparse; ignores order\nTF-IDF - tf x log(N/df); downweights common words; strong baseline with logistic regression/linear SVM\nN-grams - Capture short phrases\nWord2Vec / GloVe / fastText - Static dense embeddings; fastText handles subwords/typos\nContextual embeddings (BERT etc.) - Word meaning depends on context\nSentence embeddings - For semantic search, clustering (sentence-transformers)\n\nTasks: sentiment/text classification, NER, topic modeling (LDA, BERTopic), summarization, QA, semantic search, translation, intent detection.\n\nIndian context: code-mixed text (Hinglish), transliteration, multilingual models (IndicBERT, MuRIL, mBERT, XLM-R), and lack of labeled data for Indic languages."},"intuitive":"<p>To let a computer read text, first chop it into pieces (words or sub-words), then turn the pieces into numbers. The oldest trick is counting words. TF-IDF improves it by giving more weight to words that are frequent in this document but rare in the others (&quot;refund&quot; is informative; &quot;the&quot; is not). Modern methods use embeddings: each word or sentence becomes a point in space where similar meanings sit close together, so &quot;cheap&quot; and &quot;affordable&quot; are neighbours even though they share no letters.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Reviews, tickets and resumes are text; a TF-IDF baseline is often surprisingly hard to beat.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Start simple with TF-IDF and a linear model; move to embeddings when meaning matters more than keywords.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Classical NLP turns text into numbers. The pipeline is clean the text, tokenise, optionally remove stop words and stem, then vectorise. Bag of words counts words; TF-IDF weights a word by how often it appears in a document times how rare it is across documents, so common words get low weight and distinctive ones high. These are sparse and ignore order and meaning. Embeddings such as word2vec place words in a dense space where similar meanings are close. Contextual embeddings from transformers give a word a different vector depending on the sentence, so &#39;bank&#39; in river bank and bank loan differ. The catch: TF-IDF with logistic regression is a strong, cheap baseline, so I start there.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Turn sentences into vectors and compare them","html":"<p>Two reviews: A = &quot;good food fast delivery&quot;, B = &quot;fast delivery good service&quot;.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Vocabulary:</strong> good, food, fast, delivery, service.</li>\n<li><strong>Bag-of-words counts:</strong> A = [1, 1, 1, 1, 0]; B = [1, 0, 1, 1, 1].</li>\n<li><strong>Dot product:</strong> 1 + 0 + 1 + 1 + 0 = 3 (the shared words: good, fast, delivery).</li>\n<li><strong>Lengths:</strong> |A| = √4 = 2 and |B| = √4 = 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Cosine similarity</strong> = 3 / (2 × 2) = <strong>0.75</strong>. Quite similar, which is fair.</li>\n<li><strong>Where counting fails.</strong> C = &quot;not good food&quot;. Adding &quot;not&quot; to the vocabulary, A = [good 1, food 1, fast 1, delivery 1, not 0] and C = [1, 1, 0, 0, 1]. The dot product is 2, |C| = √3, so cosine = 2 / (2 × 1.73) ≈ <strong>0.58</strong>, a fairly high similarity to a positive review, although C is negative.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Bag-of-words compares shared words and ignores order and meaning. That is why it is a fine, cheap baseline, and why contextual embeddings that understand &quot;not good&quot; do better.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-classical-pipeline","text":"The classical pipeline","level":3},{"id":"h-representations","text":"Representations","level":3},{"id":"h-tf-idf-by-hand","text":"TF-IDF by hand","level":3},{"id":"h-tasks","text":"Tasks","level":3},{"id":"h-the-indian-context","text":"The Indian context","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Classical pipeline:</strong> lowercase -&gt; tokenize -&gt; remove stopwords/punctuation -&gt; stem (crude chop) or lemmatize (dictionary-based) -&gt; vectorize.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Representation</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bag of Words / CountVectorizer</td><td>Word counts; sparse; ignores order</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TF-IDF</strong></td><td>tf x log(N/df); downweights common words; strong baseline with logistic regression/linear SVM</td></tr><tr><td>N-grams</td><td>Capture short phrases</td></tr><tr><td>Word2Vec / GloVe / fastText</td><td>Static dense embeddings; fastText handles subwords/typos</td></tr><tr><td>Contextual embeddings (BERT etc.)</td><td>Word meaning depends on context</td></tr><tr><td>Sentence embeddings</td><td>For semantic search, clustering (sentence-transformers)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Tasks:</strong> sentiment/text classification, NER, topic modeling (LDA, BERTopic), summarization, QA, semantic search, translation, intent detection.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Indian context:</strong> code-mixed text (Hinglish), transliteration, multilingual models (IndicBERT, MuRIL, mBERT, XLM-R), and lack of labeled data for Indic languages.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-classical-pipeline\">The classical pipeline</h3>\n<p><strong>lowercase → tokenise → remove stopwords and punctuation (when helpful) → stem or lemmatise → vectorise.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stemming</strong> crudely chops suffixes (&quot;running&quot; → &quot;run&quot;, but &quot;university&quot; → &quot;univers&quot;). <strong>Lemmatisation</strong> uses a dictionary to return the proper base form (&quot;better&quot; → &quot;good&quot;).</li>\n<li>Careful with stopword removal: &quot;not good&quot; loses its negation if &quot;not&quot; is dropped.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-representations\">Representations</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Representation</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Bag of Words / CountVectorizer</strong></td><td>counts of words; sparse; ignores order</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TF-IDF</strong></td><td><code>tf × log(N / df)</code>: downweights common words; strong baseline with logistic regression or a linear SVM</td></tr><tr><td><strong>N-grams</strong></td><td>count short phrases (&quot;not good&quot;) to capture some order</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Word2Vec / GloVe / fastText</strong></td><td>static dense embeddings; fastText uses subwords, so it copes with typos and rare words</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Contextual embeddings (BERT and similar)</strong></td><td>the vector for a word depends on the sentence (&quot;bank&quot; of a river vs a bank)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sentence embeddings</strong></td><td>one vector per sentence for semantic search and clustering (sentence-transformers)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-tf-idf-by-hand\">TF-IDF by hand</h3>\n<p>Three documents: d1 &quot;the cat sat&quot;, d2 &quot;the dog sat&quot;, d3 &quot;the cat ran&quot;. N = 3.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Documents containing it (df)</th><th>idf = ln(N/df)</th><th>Comment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the</td><td>3</td><td>ln(3/3) = 0</td><td>appears everywhere, so it carries no information</td></tr><tr><td>cat</td><td>2</td><td>ln(3/2) = 0.405</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>sat</td><td>2</td><td>0.405</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>dog</td><td>1</td><td>ln(3) = 1.099</td><td>rare, so very informative</td></tr><tr><td>ran</td><td>1</td><td>1.099</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In d1 each word has tf = 1/3, so its weights are: the 0, cat 0.135, sat 0.135. In d2: the 0, dog 0.366, sat 0.135. (scikit-learn uses a smoothed variant <code>ln((1 + N)/(1 + df)) + 1</code> and L2-normalises each vector, but the idea is identical.)</p>\n<p>Explore your own documents:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"tfidf","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.feature_extraction.text <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> TfidfVectorizer\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.linear_model <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> LogisticRegression\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sklearn.pipeline <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> make_pipeline\n\nclf = make_pipeline(TfidfVectorizer(ngram_range=(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>), min_df=<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, sublinear_tf=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>), LogisticRegression(max_iter=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>))\nclf.fit(train_texts, train_labels)              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># the vectoriser is fit on TRAIN text only</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>When TF-IDF beats embeddings:</strong> short, keyword-driven tasks with lots of labelled data, domain jargon and product codes (exact tokens matter), strict latency or cost limits, and the need for interpretability (you can see which words drive a prediction). Embeddings win when meaning must generalise beyond exact words (semantic search, paraphrases, cross-lingual matching, little labelled data).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-tasks\">Tasks</h3>\n<p>Sentiment and text classification, <strong>NER</strong> (named-entity recognition), topic modelling (LDA, BERTopic), summarisation, question answering, semantic search, translation, intent detection.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-indian-context\">The Indian context</h3>\n<p><strong>Code-mixed text</strong> (Hinglish, &quot;yeh product bahut accha hai&quot;), <strong>transliteration</strong> (Hindi in Latin script, many spellings), many languages and scripts, and <strong>limited labelled data</strong> for Indic languages. Helpful models: multilingual encoders (<strong>IndicBERT, MuRIL, mBERT, XLM-R</strong>), subword tokenisers that handle spelling variation (fastText, SentencePiece), normalisation of transliterated text, and large multilingual LLMs with few-shot prompting. Always evaluate on your own noisy user text, not only clean benchmarks.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m10-nlp~1","q":"Customer-support tickets must be routed to one of 12 teams. You have 5,000 labelled tickets. What is your approach?","a":"<p>Start with a <strong>TF-IDF (word and character n-grams) + logistic regression or linear SVM</strong> baseline: fast, strong, interpretable. Evaluate macro-F1 and a per-class confusion matrix (classes may be imbalanced). Then try <strong>sentence embeddings + logistic regression</strong>, and, if the gain justifies the cost, a <strong>fine-tuned multilingual transformer</strong>. Handle Hinglish and typos (character n-grams or fastText), add metadata features (channel, product), set a confidence threshold to route uncertain tickets to humans, and monitor drift in ticket topics.</p>\n","s":"TF-IDF + logistic regression baseline first, then sentence embeddings or a fine-tuned small transformer; evaluate per class."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-nlp~ca0_1","q":"Why does TF-IDF down-weight words like &#39;the&#39;?","a":"<p>Their inverse document frequency is near zero because they appear in almost every document, so they carry no discriminating power.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-nlp~ca0_2","q":"What can embeddings do that bag of words cannot?","a":"<p>Capture similarity: &#39;great&#39; and &#39;excellent&#39; get nearby vectors, and contextual models handle word sense and order.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-nlp~ca0_3","q":"What is a sensible first model for classifying support tickets?","a":"<p>TF-IDF features plus logistic regression; evaluate it, then upgrade to fine-tuned transformers only if needed.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m10-nlp~ta1_1","q":"<p>In 100 reviews, &quot;delivery&quot; appears in 50 and &quot;soggy&quot; in 2. In one review, each word appears once among 10 words. Using tf = count / words and idf = log₁₀(100 / document count), compute TF-IDF for both words. Which is more informative?</p>\n","a":"<p>&quot;delivery&quot;: tf = 0.1, idf = log₁₀(100/50) = log₁₀ 2 ≈ 0.30, TF-IDF ≈ 0.03. &quot;soggy&quot;: tf = 0.1, idf = log₁₀(100/2) = log₁₀ 50 ≈ 1.70, TF-IDF ≈ 0.17. &quot;soggy&quot; scores about six times higher: it is rare across reviews, so it says much more about this particular one.</p>\n"}]},"m10-transformers":{"id":"m10-transformers","slug":"transformers-and-attention","m":10,"title":"Transformers and attention","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":["m10-dl-fundamentals","m10-nlp"],"tags":["nlp","transformers","attention"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Self-attention explained with a numerical example, the transformer architecture, encoder vs decoder models, and pre-training and fine-tuning.","keyTerms":["self-attention","queries keys values","softmax","scaled dot product","multi-head attention","positional encoding","BERT","GPT","encoder-decoder","pre-training","fine-tuning"],"interviewQs":["Explain self-attention in two minutes.","Why did transformers replace RNNs?","What is the difference between BERT and GPT?","Why divide by the square root of d_k?"],"mistakes":["Saying attention \"looks at every word equally\"","Forgetting positional encoding","Mixing up encoder-only and decoder-only models"],"practice":"Compute attention weights by hand for three tokens with 2-dimensional vectors, then verify with numpy.","related":["m10-dl-fundamentals","m10-llm-basics","m10-nlp"],"points":"Transformers (be able to explain in 2 minutes):\n\n- Self-attention: each token computes queries, keys, values; attention weights = softmax(QK^T / sqrt(d_k)); output = weighted sum of values. Captures long-range dependencies in parallel (vs sequential RNNs).\n- Positional encoding injects order. Multi-head attention learns different relations.\n- Encoder-only (BERT): understanding tasks. Decoder-only (GPT): generation. Encoder-decoder (T5): seq2seq.\n- Pre-train then fine-tune; masked language modeling vs next-token prediction."},"intuitive":"<p>Reading a sentence, you understand each word by looking at the other words around it: in &quot;The animal didn&#39;t cross the street because it was too tired&quot;, &quot;it&quot; means the animal. Self-attention lets every word look at every other word and decide how much to borrow from each. Each word asks a question (query), every word advertises what it offers (key), and the best matches pass along their content (value). Because all words look at each other at the same time, transformers process a sentence in parallel, unlike older models that read one word at a time.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Transformers power BERT, GPT, and every modern LLM; &quot;explain attention&quot; is a standard question.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> attention weights = softmax(QKᵀ/√d); output = weighted sum of values.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A transformer processes a whole sequence at once using self-attention. For each token, the model forms a query, key and value. The score between two tokens is the dot product of one&#39;s query with the other&#39;s key, scaled and passed through softmax to get weights; the token&#39;s new representation is the weighted sum of the values. So each token looks at the tokens most relevant to it, however far away. Multi-head attention runs several of these in parallel, and positional encodings add order information. Unlike RNNs, it parallelises on GPUs and handles long-range dependencies. The catch: attention cost grows with the square of the sequence length, which limits context size.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Attention for one word, with tiny vectors","html":"<p>The word &quot;it&quot; decides which earlier words to pay attention to. Its query is q = [1, 0]. The keys are: &quot;animal&quot; [2, 0], &quot;street&quot; [0, 2], &quot;it&quot; [1, 1]. The values are: &quot;animal&quot; [1, 0], &quot;street&quot; [0, 1], &quot;it&quot; [0.5, 0.5].</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Scores = query · key:</strong> animal 2, street 0, it 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Scale by √d</strong> (here d = 2, √2 ≈ 1.41): 1.41, 0, 0.71. Scaling stops large dot products from making softmax too sharp.</li>\n<li><strong>Softmax turns scores into weights that sum to 1:</strong> e^1.41 ≈ 4.11, e^0 = 1, e^0.71 ≈ 2.03; total 7.14. Weights: animal <strong>0.58</strong>, street <strong>0.14</strong>, it <strong>0.28</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Output = weighted average of the values:</strong> 0.58 × [1, 0] + 0.14 × [0, 1] + 0.28 × [0.5, 0.5] = <strong>[0.72, 0.28]</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Read it.</strong> The new representation of &quot;it&quot; is mostly &quot;animal&quot;: attention has resolved what &quot;it&quot; refers to.</li>\n<li><strong>In a real model</strong> the queries, keys and values are learned projections of each word&#39;s embedding, every word does this for every other word at once, and several heads run in parallel.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Attention is a weighted average, where the weights come from how well one word&#39;s query matches the others&#39; keys.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-self-attention-step-by-step","text":"Self-attention, step by step","level":3},{"id":"h-the-rest-of-the-architecture","text":"The rest of the architecture","level":3},{"id":"h-model-families","text":"Model families","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Transformers (be able to explain in 2 minutes):</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Self-attention:</strong> each token computes queries, keys, values; attention weights = softmax(QK^T / sqrt(d_k)); output = weighted sum of values. Captures long-range dependencies in parallel (vs sequential RNNs).</li>\n<li><strong>Positional encoding</strong> injects order. <strong>Multi-head</strong> attention learns different relations.</li>\n<li><strong>Encoder-only (BERT):</strong> understanding tasks. <strong>Decoder-only (GPT):</strong> generation. <strong>Encoder-decoder (T5):</strong> seq2seq.</li>\n<li><strong>Pre-train then fine-tune</strong>; masked language modeling vs next-token prediction.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> transformers <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> pipeline\nclf = pipeline(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sentiment-analysis&quot;</span>)\nclf(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The delivery was super fast but the packaging was poor.&quot;</span>)</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-self-attention-step-by-step\">Self-attention, step by step</h3>\n<p>Each token&#39;s embedding is projected into a <strong>query</strong> <code>Q</code>, <strong>key</strong> <code>K</code> and <strong>value</strong> <code>V</code>. For a whole sequence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Attention</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext>softmax</mtext><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">⊤</mi></msup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\text{softmax}\\!\\left(\\frac{QK^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) V.</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>\n<ol>\n<li><code>QKᵀ</code>: a score for every pair of tokens (how well a token&#39;s query matches another&#39;s key).</li>\n<li>Divide by <code>√d_k</code> so large dot products do not push the softmax into saturation (whose gradients vanish).</li>\n<li><strong>Softmax</strong> turns each row into weights that sum to 1.</li>\n<li>Multiply by <code>V</code>: each token&#39;s output is a <strong>weighted average of all value vectors</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>A tiny example</strong> with three tokens and 2-D embeddings (and, for simplicity, <code>Q = K = V = E</code>): &quot;the&quot; = (1, 0), &quot;cat&quot; = (0, 1), &quot;sat&quot; = (1, 1).</p>\n<p>Scores <code>E·Eᵀ / √2</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>the</th><th>cat</th><th>sat</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the</td><td>0.71</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.71</td></tr><tr><td>cat</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.71</td><td>0.71</td></tr><tr><td>sat</td><td>0.71</td><td>0.71</td><td>1.41</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>After softmax (each row sums to 1):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>the</th><th>cat</th><th>sat</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>the</td><td>0.40</td><td>0.20</td><td>0.40</td></tr><tr><td>cat</td><td>0.20</td><td>0.40</td><td>0.40</td></tr><tr><td>sat</td><td>0.25</td><td>0.25</td><td>0.50</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The output for &quot;the&quot; is <code>0.4·(1,0) + 0.2·(0,1) + 0.4·(1,1) = (0.8, 0.6)</code>: a blend of itself and its context. Change the tokens and temperature yourself:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"attention","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">attention</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">Q, K, V</span>):\n    scores = Q @ K.T / np.sqrt(K.shape[-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>])\n    w = np.exp(scores - scores.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">max</span>(-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, keepdims=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)); w /= w.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>(-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, keepdims=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> w @ V, w</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-rest-of-the-architecture\">The rest of the architecture</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Multi-head attention:</strong> several attention &quot;heads&quot; in parallel with different projections, each learning a different relationship (syntax, coreference, position); outputs are concatenated.</li>\n<li><strong>Positional encoding:</strong> attention itself ignores order, so position information (sinusoidal or learned; rotary embeddings are common now) is added to the token embeddings.</li>\n<li><strong>Feed-forward layers, residual connections and layer norm</strong> in every block; stack many blocks.</li>\n<li><strong>Why they beat RNNs:</strong> full parallelism across the sequence (fast training on GPUs), direct connections between distant tokens (no fading memory), and they scale well with data and compute. The cost: attention is <strong>O(n²)</strong> in sequence length.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-model-families\">Model families</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th><th>Training objective</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Encoder-only</strong></td><td>BERT</td><td>masked language modelling (predict hidden words)</td><td>understanding: classification, NER, embeddings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Decoder-only</strong></td><td>GPT-style LLMs</td><td>next-token prediction (causal mask: each token sees only the past)</td><td>generation, chat, agents</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Encoder-decoder</strong></td><td>T5, BART</td><td>sequence-to-sequence</td><td>translation, summarisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Pre-train then fine-tune:</strong> learn general language from huge unlabelled text, then adapt to a task with a small labelled set (or parameter-efficient methods such as LoRA).</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> transformers <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> pipeline\nclf = pipeline(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sentiment-analysis&quot;</span>)\nclf(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;The delivery was super fast but the packaging was poor.&quot;</span>)     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [{&#x27;label&#x27;: &#x27;NEGATIVE&#x27; or &#x27;POSITIVE&#x27;, &#x27;score&#x27;: ...}]  (mixed review: the score will be less extreme)</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m10-transformers~1","q":"Why does self-attention divide the scores by √d_k?","a":"<p>If query and key components have unit variance, their dot product has variance <code>d_k</code>, so for large <code>d_k</code> the scores become large in magnitude. The softmax then outputs near one-hot distributions with tiny gradients, making training unstable. Scaling by <code>1/√d_k</code> keeps the variance near 1.</p>\n","s":"To keep the dot products from growing with dimension, which would saturate the softmax and kill gradients."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-transformers~ca0_1","q":"In one sentence, what does self-attention compute?","a":"<p>For each token, a weighted average of all tokens&#39; value vectors, with weights from how well its query matches their keys.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-transformers~ca0_2","q":"Why do transformers need positional encodings?","a":"<p>Attention itself is order-agnostic; position information must be added so &#39;dog bites man&#39; differs from &#39;man bites dog&#39;.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-transformers~ca0_3","q":"Main advantage over RNNs?","a":"<p>Parallel training across the sequence and direct long-range connections, avoiding sequential bottlenecks and vanishing gradients.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m10-transformers~ta1_1","q":"<p>Repeat the calculation with the query q = [0, 1] (the same keys and values). Which word gets the most weight, and what is the output vector?</p>\n","a":"<p>Scores: animal 0, street 2, it 1. Scaled: 0, 1.41, 0.71. Softmax weights: animal 0.14, street 0.58, it 0.28. Output = 0.14 × [1, 0] + 0.58 × [0, 1] + 0.28 × [0.5, 0.5] = [0.28, 0.72]. Now &quot;street&quot; dominates. The same keys and values give a different result for a different query, which is how each word gathers the context it needs.</p>\n"}]},"m10-llm-basics":{"id":"m10-llm-basics","slug":"llm-fundamentals-and-prompting","m":10,"title":"LLM fundamentals and prompting","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":["m10-transformers"],"tags":["genai","llm","prompting"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"LLM fundamentals (tokens, context, sampling parameters, training stages, hallucination) and prompting techniques, with practical guidance.","keyTerms":["tokens","tokenization","context window","embeddings","temperature","top-p","pre-training","supervised fine-tuning","RLHF","hallucination","few-shot","chain-of-thought"],"interviewQs":["What is temperature and how does it change output?","How do LLMs get trained (pre-training, SFT, RLHF/DPO)?","What is hallucination and how do you reduce it?","Zero-shot vs few-shot vs chain-of-thought prompting?"],"mistakes":["Using a high temperature for factual tasks","Assuming the model knows up-to-date or private facts","Prompts without clear format or examples"],"practice":"Write a prompt that extracts three fields from a support email as JSON, test it on five tricky inputs and refine it.","related":["m10-transformers","m10-rag","m10-genai-more"],"points":"Fundamentals\n\n- Tokens and tokenization (BPE), context window, embeddings, next-token prediction, sampling parameters (temperature, top-p, top-k, max tokens).\n- Training stages: pre-training -> supervised fine-tuning -> preference tuning (RLHF/DPO).\n- Hallucination: fluent but wrong output; mitigations: grounding with retrieval, citations, constrained outputs, verification, lower temperature.\n\nPrompting\n\n- Zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought, role/system prompts, structured outputs (JSON schema), delimiters, iterative prompt evaluation."},"intuitive":"<p>A large language model is a very well-read autocomplete. It has read a huge amount of text and learned to predict the next small piece of text (a token) that would most plausibly come next. Give it a prompt, and it keeps predicting the next piece, one at a time. It does not look facts up; it writes what sounds right, which is why it can confidently make things up (hallucinate). You steer it with clear instructions, examples and by giving it the facts it needs, and control how adventurous it is with the temperature setting.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> GenAI questions are increasingly common in data science interviews.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Tokens in, next-token probabilities out; steer with prompts and context; ground facts with retrieval.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A large language model is a transformer trained to predict the next token on huge text. Text is split into tokens, pieces of words. At generation, the model outputs a probability over the next token, and sampling settings control creativity: temperature near zero is near-deterministic, higher is more varied; top-p limits sampling to the most likely tokens. After pretraining, instruction tuning and human feedback make it follow instructions. The context window is the amount of text it can see at once. Prompting works better with a clear role, explicit task, examples, and the output format. The catch: LLMs hallucinate, producing fluent but false statements, so I ground them with retrieved sources and validate outputs.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"What temperature does to the next word","html":"<p>After &quot;The capital of France is&quot;, a model&#39;s raw scores (logits) are: Paris 5, Lyon 2, &quot;a&quot; 1. Probabilities come from softmax(logits / temperature).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Temperature 1:</strong> e⁵ ≈ 148.4, e² ≈ 7.4, e¹ ≈ 2.7. Probabilities: Paris <strong>0.94</strong>, Lyon 0.05, &quot;a&quot; 0.02.</li>\n<li><strong>Temperature 0.5</strong> (the logits double to 10, 4, 2): Paris <strong>0.997</strong>. Nearly deterministic: the top word almost always wins.</li>\n<li><strong>Temperature 2</strong> (the logits halve to 2.5, 1, 0.5): Paris <strong>0.74</strong>, Lyon 0.16, &quot;a&quot; 0.10. More variety, and more chances to pick something wrong.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Temperature</th><th>Paris</th><th>Lyon</th><th>&quot;a&quot;</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.5</td><td>0.997</td><td>0.002</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.94</td><td>0.05</td><td>0.02</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.74</td><td>0.16</td><td>0.10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li><strong>Choose by task.</strong> Extracting facts, writing SQL or classifying: low temperature (around 0 to 0.3). Brainstorming names or marketing copy: higher (0.7 to 1).</li>\n<li><strong>Hallucination link.</strong> At any temperature, the model picks <em>plausible</em> text. If the right fact is not well represented, a fluent wrong answer can still have the highest probability. Grounding with retrieved sources (RAG) addresses that; temperature does not.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The model produces a probability for every next token; temperature sharpens or flattens that distribution before sampling.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-fundamentals","text":"Fundamentals","level":3},{"id":"h-training-stages","text":"Training stages","level":3},{"id":"h-hallucination","text":"Hallucination","level":3},{"id":"h-prompting-techniques","text":"Prompting techniques","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Fundamentals</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Tokens and tokenization (BPE), context window, embeddings, next-token prediction, sampling parameters (<strong>temperature</strong>, top-p, top-k, max tokens).</li>\n<li><strong>Training stages:</strong> pre-training -&gt; supervised fine-tuning -&gt; preference tuning (RLHF/DPO).</li>\n<li><strong>Hallucination:</strong> fluent but wrong output; mitigations: grounding with retrieval, citations, constrained outputs, verification, lower temperature.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Prompting</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Zero-shot, few-shot, chain-of-thought, role/system prompts, structured outputs (JSON schema), delimiters, iterative prompt evaluation.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-fundamentals\">Fundamentals</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tokens and tokenisation:</strong> text is split into sub-word pieces (Byte-Pair Encoding and similar). Roughly 4 characters of English is a token; Indic scripts often need more tokens per word, which raises cost and uses more context.</li>\n<li><strong>Context window:</strong> the maximum tokens (prompt + output) the model can consider at once.</li>\n<li><strong>Embeddings:</strong> vector representations of tokens or text used for similarity search.</li>\n<li><strong>Generation:</strong> the model outputs a probability distribution over the next token; a <strong>sampling</strong> strategy picks one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sampling parameters</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Temperature</strong></td><td>scales logits before softmax; low (0 to 0.3) is focused and repeatable, high is more diverse and creative. For logits [2, 1, 0.1]: T = 0.5 gives probabilities [0.86, 0.12, 0.02]; T = 1 gives [0.66, 0.24, 0.10]; T = 2 gives [0.50, 0.30, 0.19]</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Top-p (nucleus)</strong></td><td>sample only from the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability ≥ p</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Top-k</strong></td><td>sample only from the k most likely tokens</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Max tokens</strong></td><td>output length cap</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Use low temperature for extraction, classification and factual answers; moderate for drafting and brainstorming.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-training-stages\">Training stages</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pre-training:</strong> next-token prediction on huge text corpora, building general knowledge and language ability.</li>\n<li><strong>Supervised fine-tuning (SFT):</strong> train on curated instruction-response pairs so the model follows instructions.</li>\n<li><strong>Preference tuning (RLHF, DPO and relatives):</strong> align outputs with human preferences (helpfulness, safety), using comparisons of better vs worse answers.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-hallucination\">Hallucination</h3>\n<p>Fluent but wrong or unsupported output. It arises because the model optimises plausible continuations, not truth, and has no built-in fact lookup or knowledge of recent or private information. <strong>Mitigations:</strong> ground answers in <strong>retrieved documents with citations</strong> (RAG), constrain the output format and allowed values, lower the temperature, ask the model to say &quot;I don&#39;t know&quot; when the context lacks the answer, verify with tools or a second check, and evaluate with golden test sets.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-prompting-techniques\">Prompting techniques</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>What</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Zero-shot</strong></td><td>instruction only</td><td>simple, well-known tasks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Few-shot</strong></td><td>add 2 to 5 worked examples</td><td>you need a specific format or style</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Chain-of-thought</strong></td><td>ask for step-by-step reasoning (or use a reasoning model)</td><td>multi-step logic or maths</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Role / system prompt</strong></td><td>set persona, constraints and policies</td><td>consistent behaviour</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Structured output</strong></td><td>request JSON matching a schema; validate it</td><td>integrate with code</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Delimiters</strong></td><td>mark instructions vs data (<code>&quot;&quot;&quot;...&quot;&quot;&quot;</code>, XML tags)</td><td>avoid confusion and reduce prompt injection</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">You extract fields from support emails. Return ONLY valid JSON with keys: issue_type (one of &quot;delivery&quot;,&quot;payment&quot;,&quot;product&quot;,&quot;other&quot;), urgency (1-5), order_id (string or null).\nEmail: &quot;&quot;&quot;{email_text}&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Treat prompts like code: version them, test against a fixed set of examples (a <strong>golden set</strong>), and measure changes rather than eyeballing single outputs.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m10-llm-basics~1","q":"An LLM-powered assistant confidently quotes a refund policy that does not exist. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Put the real policy documents behind a <strong>retrieval step</strong> (RAG), instruct the model to answer <strong>only from the provided context</strong> and cite the passage (or say it does not know), keep temperature low, and validate that cited text actually supports the answer. Add an evaluation set of policy questions (including unanswerable ones) to measure <strong>faithfulness</strong> and refusal behaviour, and log failures for continuous improvement.</p>\n","s":"Ground it: retrieve the actual policy text, instruct the model to answer only from it with citations, and evaluate for faithfulness."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-llm-basics~ca0_1","q":"Temperature 0 versus temperature 1?","a":"<p>Near 0 picks the most likely token each time (stable, repetitive); 1 samples more freely (varied, riskier).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-llm-basics~ca0_2","q":"Why do LLMs hallucinate?","a":"<p>They are trained to produce plausible next tokens, not verified facts; without grounding they will fill gaps convincingly.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-llm-basics~ca0_3","q":"Three prompt-quality tips?","a":"<p>Specify the role and task clearly, give examples (few-shot), and define the output format; ask for reasoning or citations when useful.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m10-llm-basics~ta1_1","q":"<p>A support bot will answer 2,000 questions a day. Each call sends a 1,200-token prompt (instructions plus retrieved passages) and receives a 300-token answer. How many tokens a day is that, and name two ways to cut it.</p>\n","a":"<p>2,000 × (1,200 + 300) = 3,000,000 tokens a day. To cut it: retrieve fewer or shorter chunks (for example the top 3 instead of the top 6) and shorten the instructions; cache answers to frequent questions; route simple questions to a smaller, cheaper model; and cap the answer length. Measure answer quality on an evaluation set after each cut.</p>\n"}]},"m10-rag":{"id":"m10-rag","slug":"rag-end-to-end","m":10,"title":"RAG end to end","min":40,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":["m10-llm-basics"],"tags":["genai","rag","retrieval"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Retrieval-augmented generation end to end: ingestion, chunking, embedding, retrieval, reranking, prompting, evaluation and the failure modes to expect, with a minimal implementation.","keyTerms":["chunking","embeddings","vector database","hybrid search","BM25","reranking","faithfulness","recall at k","prompt injection","lost in the middle"],"interviewQs":["Draw and explain a RAG architecture.","How do you choose chunk size?","How do you evaluate a RAG system?","Name three failure modes and how you would fix them."],"mistakes":["Evaluating only the final answer and not the retrieval step","Chunks that cut through sentences or tables","Trusting retrieved text blindly (prompt injection)"],"practice":"Build a small RAG over five PDFs with a golden set of 30 questions and measure retrieval recall@k and answer faithfulness.","related":["m10-llm-basics","m10-genai-more","m9-recommenders"],"points":"RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation): know end to end\n\n- Design decisions: chunking strategy, embedding model, vector store (FAISS, Chroma, Pinecone, Qdrant, pgvector), top-k, hybrid search, reranking, metadata filters, prompt template, guardrails.\n- Evaluation: retrieval (recall@k, MRR, hit rate), generation (faithfulness/groundedness, answer relevance, context precision/recall; frameworks like RAGAS, LLM-as-judge with human spot checks), latency and cost.\n- Failure modes: bad chunking, irrelevant retrieval, lost-in-the-middle, stale index, prompt injection from documents."},"intuitive":"<p>RAG is an open-book exam for an AI. Instead of asking the model to answer from memory, you first look up the most relevant pages from your own documents, then hand those pages to the model and say &quot;answer using only these&quot;. To make the lookup work, you cut documents into chunks, turn each chunk into a vector of numbers that captures its meaning, and store them. When a question arrives, you turn it into a vector too and fetch the closest chunks. Most mistakes come from the lookup (wrong pages found), not from the writing.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> It is the dominant pattern for company GenAI apps (policies, reports, knowledge bases) and a top project idea.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Good RAG is good retrieval; evaluate retrieval and answers separately.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Retrieval-augmented generation gives an LLM relevant documents at question time. Offline: split documents into chunks, turn each into an embedding vector, and store them in a vector database. Online: embed the question, retrieve the nearest chunks by similarity, put them in the prompt with an instruction to answer only from them, and generate with citations. It reduces hallucination, uses fresh private data, and needs no retraining. Quality hinges on chunk size, good embeddings, hybrid search with keywords, and reranking. I evaluate retrieval separately, with recall at K, and the answer for faithfulness to the sources. The catch: if retrieval fails, the answer is wrong, so I debug retrieval first.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Retrieval by cosine similarity, then the prompt","html":"<p>Question embedding q = [0.9, 0.1, 0.4]. Three chunks in the index:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Chunk</th><th>Embedding</th><th>Cosine with q</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Refund policy</td><td>[0.8, 0.2, 0.5]</td><td><strong>0.985</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Shipping times</td><td>[0.1, 0.9, 0.3]</td><td>0.318</td></tr><tr><td>Warranty terms</td><td>[0.6, 0.1, 0.8]</td><td>0.874</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Embed the question</strong> with the <em>same</em> model that embedded the chunks.</li>\n<li><strong>Score every chunk</strong> by cosine similarity (an approximate-nearest-neighbour index does this quickly for millions of chunks).</li>\n<li><strong>Take the top k = 2:</strong> Refund policy and Warranty terms.</li>\n<li><strong>Build the prompt:</strong>\n<em>&quot;Answer using only the sources below. If the answer is not in them, say you do not know. Cite the source title.\n[Refund policy] ...\n[Warranty terms] ...\nQuestion: Can I return a phone after 20 days?&quot;</em></li>\n<li><strong>Generate</strong> with a low temperature, then check that the answer cites a source.</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluate both halves separately.</strong> Retrieval: was the right chunk in the top k (recall@k)? Generation: is every claim supported by the retrieved text (faithfulness)?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> RAG is a search step followed by a constrained writing step. When an answer is wrong, first check whether the right chunk was retrieved.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-pipeline","text":"The pipeline","level":3},{"id":"h-a-minimal-implementation","text":"A minimal implementation","level":3},{"id":"h-evaluation-two-levels","text":"Evaluation: two levels","level":3},{"id":"h-failure-modes-and-fixes","text":"Failure modes and fixes","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation): know end to end</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">Documents -&gt; clean/parse -&gt; chunk (size 300-800 tokens, overlap) -&gt; embed -&gt; vector DB\nQuery -&gt; embed -&gt; retrieve top-k (hybrid: BM25 + dense) -&gt; (optional) rerank -&gt; build prompt with context -&gt; LLM -&gt; answer with citations</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Design decisions:</strong> chunking strategy, embedding model, vector store (FAISS, Chroma, Pinecone, Qdrant, pgvector), top-k, hybrid search, reranking, metadata filters, prompt template, guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluation:</strong> retrieval (recall@k, MRR, hit rate), generation (faithfulness/groundedness, answer relevance, context precision/recall; frameworks like RAGAS, LLM-as-judge with human spot checks), latency and cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> bad chunking, irrelevant retrieval, lost-in-the-middle, stale index, prompt injection from documents.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-pipeline\">The pipeline</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">Documents -&gt; clean/parse -&gt; chunk (300-800 tokens, with overlap) -&gt; embed -&gt; vector DB\nQuery -&gt; embed -&gt; retrieve top-k (hybrid: BM25 + dense) -&gt; (optional) rerank -&gt; build prompt with context -&gt; LLM -&gt; answer with citations</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Design decisions and how to think about them</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Guidance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Chunking</strong></td><td>300 to 800 tokens with 10 to 20% overlap is a typical start; respect structure (headings, paragraphs, tables, clauses); smaller chunks improve precision, larger chunks keep context. Test against your questions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Embedding model</strong></td><td>multilingual if your content is Hindi or code-mixed; evaluate on your own queries</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Vector store</strong></td><td>FAISS (library), Chroma, Qdrant, Pinecone, <code>pgvector</code> (inside Postgres); choose by scale, filtering needs and ops</td></tr><tr><td><strong>top-k</strong></td><td>retrieve more (for example 20), then <strong>rerank</strong> down to 3 to 5</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hybrid search</strong></td><td>combine <strong>BM25</strong> (keyword, good for codes and names) with dense vectors (meaning), for example by reciprocal rank fusion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reranking</strong></td><td>a <strong>cross-encoder</strong> scores (query, chunk) pairs jointly; more accurate but slower</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metadata filters</strong></td><td>restrict by document type, date, product, user permission</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prompt template</strong></td><td>instruct: answer only from context; cite source ids; say &quot;not found&quot; if missing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guardrails</strong></td><td>PII masking, refusal policies, output validation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-minimal-implementation\">A minimal implementation</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> sentence_transformers <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> SentenceTransformer\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> faiss, numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sentence-transformers/paraphrase-multilingual-MiniLM-L12-v2&quot;</span>)\nchunks = split_into_chunks(documents, size=<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>, overlap=<span class=\"hljs-number\">80</span>)              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># your chunker</span>\nemb = model.encode(chunks, normalize_embeddings=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)                    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># unit vectors: dot product = cosine similarity</span>\nindex = faiss.IndexFlatIP(emb.shape[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]); index.add(np.asarray(emb, dtype=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;float32&quot;</span>))\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">retrieve</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">question, k=<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span></span>):\n    q = model.encode([question], normalize_embeddings=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)\n    scores, ids = index.search(np.asarray(q, dtype=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;float32&quot;</span>), k)\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> [(chunks[i], <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">float</span>(s)) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i, s <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">zip</span>(ids[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>], scores[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>])]\n\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">build_prompt</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">question</span>):\n    ctx = <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\\n\\n&quot;</span>.join(<span class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;[<span class=\"hljs-subst\">{i + <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>}</span>] <span class=\"hljs-subst\">{c}</span>&quot;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> i, (c, _) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">enumerate</span>(retrieve(question)))\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">return</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;Answer using ONLY the context. Cite sources like [1]. If the answer is not in the context, say you do not know.\\n\\nContext:\\n<span class=\"hljs-subst\">{ctx}</span>\\n\\nQuestion: <span class=\"hljs-subst\">{question}</span>&quot;</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-evaluation-two-levels\">Evaluation: two levels</h3>\n<p><strong>Retrieval:</strong> recall@k (is the needed chunk in the top k?), MRR, hit rate, context precision/recall. <strong>Generation:</strong> <strong>faithfulness / groundedness</strong> (every claim supported by the context), answer relevance, completeness. Build a <strong>golden set</strong> (for example 30 to 100 questions with the expected source passages and answers, including unanswerable questions), automate it (RAGAS-style metrics, <strong>LLM-as-judge</strong> with periodic human spot-checks), and track latency and cost.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-failure-modes-and-fixes\">Failure modes and fixes</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Failure</th><th>Symptom</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Bad chunking</strong></td><td>answer split across chunks; tables mangled</td><td>structure-aware chunking, larger overlap, parent-child chunks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Irrelevant retrieval</strong></td><td>wrong context, confident wrong answer</td><td>hybrid search, reranker, query rewriting, metadata filters, better embeddings</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lost in the middle</strong></td><td>the model ignores relevant context buried in a long prompt</td><td>fewer, better chunks; put the best first; rerank</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stale index</strong></td><td>outdated answers</td><td>scheduled re-ingestion, versioning, deletions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prompt injection from documents</strong></td><td>retrieved text says &quot;ignore previous instructions&quot;</td><td>treat retrieved text as data, delimit it, restrict tool permissions, filter and monitor</td></tr><tr><td><strong>No &quot;I don&#39;t know&quot; behaviour</strong></td><td>fabricated answers when nothing relevant was retrieved</td><td>score thresholds, explicit refusal instruction, eval with unanswerable questions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m10-rag~1","q":"Your RAG bot answers incorrectly on 20% of test questions. How do you find the cause?","a":"<p>For each failed question, inspect the retrieved chunks. If the <strong>needed passage was not retrieved</strong>, it is a retrieval problem: improve chunking, add hybrid search or reranking, tune top-k, rewrite queries, fix metadata filters. If the passage <strong>was retrieved but the answer was wrong</strong>, it is a generation problem: improve the prompt (answer only from context, cite), reduce context noise, use a stronger model, or add a verification step. Fix the larger bucket first and track recall@k and faithfulness separately so you can see which change helped.</p>\n","s":"Split failures into retrieval misses and generation errors by checking whether the right chunk was in the context."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-rag~ca0_1","q":"The RAG bot gives a wrong answer. What do you check first?","a":"<p>Whether the right chunk was retrieved (retrieval recall). If so, check the prompt and generation; if not, fix chunking, embeddings, or add hybrid search and reranking.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-rag~ca0_2","q":"Why chunk documents?","a":"<p>Embeddings and prompts have limits and a whole document dilutes meaning; smaller focused chunks retrieve precisely and fit the context window.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-rag~ca0_3","q":"Name two benefits of RAG over relying on the model alone.","a":"<p>Up-to-date or private knowledge without retraining, and answers traceable to sources, which reduces hallucination.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m10-rag~ta1_1","q":"<p>For 50 test questions, the chunk containing the answer ranks 1st for 30, 2nd or 3rd for 10, between 4th and 10th for 6, and is not found for 4. With k = 3, what is recall@3? Name two fixes for the misses.</p>\n","a":"<p>Recall@3 = (30 + 10) / 50 = 0.8. Ten questions never reach the model with the right context. Fixes: raise k and add a reranker so that the relevant chunk moves up (it helps the 6 at ranks 4 to 10); improve chunking, for example smaller chunks or keeping headings with their text; and add hybrid keyword search for exact terms such as product codes, which often catches the 4 that were not found.</p>\n"}]},"m10-genai-more":{"id":"m10-genai-more","slug":"rag-vs-fine-tuning-agents-and-project-ideas","m":10,"title":"RAG vs fine-tuning, agents and project ideas","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":["m10-rag"],"tags":["genai","agents","fine-tuning"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"When to use RAG versus fine-tuning, an overview of agents and tool calling, evaluation, cost and latency optimisation, safety, and GenAI project ideas for a portfolio.","keyTerms":["RAG vs fine-tuning","LoRA","agents","tool calling","function calling","guardrails","cost and latency","golden sets","project ideas"],"interviewQs":["RAG or fine-tuning for a company policy assistant?","What is tool (function) calling and how do agents work?","How do you reduce LLM cost and latency?"],"mistakes":["Fine-tuning to inject facts that change often","Giving agents broad permissions","Shipping without an evaluation set"],"practice":"Design a text-to-SQL assistant: list components, guardrails, evaluation and failure modes.","related":["m10-rag","m10-llm-basics","m14-portfolio"],"points":"RAG vs fine-tuning\n\nNeed - Prefer\nFresh/private knowledge, citations, frequent updates - RAG\nStyle, format, domain behavior, latency/cost at scale - Fine-tuning (LoRA/QLoRA, parameter-efficient)\nBoth - RAG + light fine-tuning\n\nOther topics: agents and tool/function calling, embeddings and cosine similarity, LangChain/LlamaIndex basics, evaluation datasets (\"golden sets\"), guardrails/PII handling, cost and latency optimization (caching, smaller models, batching), prompt injection and safety.\n\nProject-ready ideas: document Q&A over policies/annual reports, text-to-SQL assistant, resume screener, support-ticket triage, review summarizer."},"intuitive":"<p>There are two main ways to make an AI know your stuff. You can hand it a reference book each time (RAG: great for facts that change and need citations), or you can send it to school on your examples so its habits change (fine-tuning: great for a consistent style, format or specialist behaviour). Agents go one step further: the model can decide to use tools, like running a database query or a calculator, look at the result, and continue. The more power you give it, the more you must test, limit and monitor it.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers ask about trade-offs (RAG vs fine-tuning), safety and evaluation, not about API syntax.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Facts via retrieval, behaviour via fine-tuning, actions via tools with guardrails, all measured on a golden set.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;When should I use RAG and when fine-tuning? RAG adds knowledge: facts that change or are private. Fine-tuning changes behaviour: tone, format, or a specialised skill. They combine well. Start with prompting, add RAG for knowledge, and fine-tune last, when needed for consistent style or latency and cost. An agent is an LLM that can plan and call tools, like search, a database or code, in a loop until the task is done. It is powerful but less predictable, so I add limits, logging and human checks. Good project ideas: a document question-answering bot, a support-ticket triage tool, or a SQL assistant. The catch: evaluate with a test set of real questions, not impressions.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Prompt, RAG or fine-tune: decide case by case","html":"<p>Four requests from the business.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>&quot;Summarise customer calls in our standard three-bullet format.&quot;</strong> The knowledge is in the call itself; only the format matters. <strong>Prompting</strong> with two or three examples. Fine-tune only if the format still drifts across thousands of calls.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Answer employee questions from the HR policy, which changes every quarter.&quot;</strong> The knowledge is private and changes. <strong>RAG</strong>: update the documents, and the answers change the same day.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Write product descriptions in our brand voice, at scale, cheaply.&quot;</strong> Behaviour and style, needed many times. <strong>Fine-tune</strong> a smaller model (for example with LoRA) on a few thousand approved descriptions, which also lowers cost per call.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Tell me last month&#39;s revenue against the month before.&quot;</strong> The facts live in a database. An <strong>agent with a SQL tool</strong>: it writes a query, runs it, checks the result and explains it, within read-only permissions.</li>\n<li><strong>For every case, write the evaluation first:</strong> 30 to 100 real examples with the expected output, scored before and after each change.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Knowledge goes in through retrieval or tools; behaviour goes in through prompts or fine-tuning. Start with the cheapest option that passes your evaluation set.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-rag-vs-fine-tuning","text":"RAG vs fine-tuning","level":3},{"id":"h-agents-and-tool-calling","text":"Agents and tool calling","level":3},{"id":"h-evaluation-and-safety","text":"Evaluation and safety","level":3},{"id":"h-cost-and-latency","text":"Cost and latency","level":3},{"id":"h-project-ready-ideas","text":"Project-ready ideas","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>RAG vs fine-tuning</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Prefer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fresh/private knowledge, citations, frequent updates</td><td><strong>RAG</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Style, format, domain behavior, latency/cost at scale</td><td><strong>Fine-tuning</strong> (LoRA/QLoRA, parameter-efficient)</td></tr><tr><td>Both</td><td>RAG + light fine-tuning</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Other topics:</strong> agents and tool/function calling, embeddings and cosine similarity, LangChain/LlamaIndex basics, evaluation datasets (&quot;golden sets&quot;), guardrails/PII handling, cost and latency optimization (caching, smaller models, batching), prompt injection and safety.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Project-ready ideas:</strong> document Q&amp;A over policies/annual reports, text-to-SQL assistant, resume screener, support-ticket triage, review summarizer.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-rag-vs-fine-tuning\">RAG vs fine-tuning</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Prefer</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fresh or private knowledge, citations, frequent updates</td><td><strong>RAG</strong></td><td>update the index instead of retraining; traceable sources</td></tr><tr><td>Style, format, tone, domain behaviour, latency and cost at scale</td><td><strong>Fine-tuning</strong> (LoRA / QLoRA, parameter-efficient)</td><td>changes behaviour; smaller prompts</td></tr><tr><td>Both</td><td>RAG + light fine-tuning</td><td>facts from retrieval, behaviour from tuning</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>LoRA</strong> fine-tunes by training small low-rank adapter matrices while the base model is frozen, cutting memory and cost; <strong>QLoRA</strong> does it on a quantised base model.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-agents-and-tool-calling\">Agents and tool calling</h3>\n<p>A model can be given <strong>tools</strong> described by name and JSON schema (search, SQL query, calculator, calendar). The model emits a structured <strong>tool call</strong>, your code executes it, and the result is fed back; the loop repeats until the model gives a final answer. Good practice: minimal permissions (read-only database user), validate arguments, limit steps and cost, log everything, require confirmation for irreversible actions, and guard against <strong>prompt injection</strong> that tries to make the agent misuse tools.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-evaluation-and-safety\">Evaluation and safety</h3>\n<p>Build <strong>golden sets</strong> of representative (and adversarial) inputs with expected outputs or rubrics; run them on every prompt, model or retrieval change; combine automatic metrics, <strong>LLM-as-judge</strong> and human review. Add <strong>guardrails</strong>: input and output filters, PII handling (mask personal data; note India&#39;s DPDP Act principles), refusal rules, schema validation, and rate limits.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-cost-and-latency\">Cost and latency</h3>\n<p>Use a <strong>smaller model</strong> where quality allows, <strong>cache</strong> repeated prompts and embeddings, shorten prompts (retrieve fewer, better chunks), <strong>batch</strong> requests, <strong>stream</strong> responses for perceived speed, route easy queries to cheap models and hard ones to strong models, and constrain output length.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-project-ready-ideas\">Project-ready ideas</h3>\n<p>Document Q&amp;A over policies or annual reports; <strong>text-to-SQL</strong> assistant (schema retrieval, SQL validation, read-only execution); resume screener; support-ticket triage; review summariser with aspect extraction. Each should include an evaluation set, a README with limitations, and a small demo.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m10-genai-more~1","q":"Design a text-to-SQL assistant for business users. What can go wrong and how do you protect against it?","a":"<p>Components: retrieve the relevant table and column descriptions (schema RAG), give a few examples, generate SQL, <strong>validate</strong> it (parse, allow only <code>SELECT</code>, whitelisted tables, row and time limits), execute with a <strong>read-only</strong> user and timeouts, and explain the result. Risks: <strong>incorrect logic</strong> that looks plausible (mitigate with an evaluation set of question-to-SQL pairs, result sanity checks, showing the SQL to the user), <strong>ambiguous questions</strong> (ask clarifying questions), <strong>data leakage across permissions</strong> (row-level security), <strong>prompt injection</strong> via column comments or data, and <strong>cost</strong>. Measure execution accuracy on the golden set and log user corrections.</p>\n","s":"Wrong or unsafe SQL and wrong schema assumptions: retrieve relevant schema, validate and sandbox queries (read-only, limits), evaluate on a golden set."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-genai-more~ca0_1","q":"Company policies change weekly and the bot must answer from them. RAG or fine-tuning?","a":"<p>RAG: update the document store and answers change immediately; fine-tuning would need retraining for each change.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-genai-more~ca0_2","q":"What makes an agent riskier than a plain prompt?","a":"<p>It takes actions and chains steps, so errors compound and side effects occur; mitigate with tool limits, validation, logging and approvals.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-genai-more~ca0_3","q":"How do you evaluate an LLM application?","a":"<p>Build a labelled set of realistic questions, score retrieval and answer faithfulness/correctness (with human or model graders), and track regressions on each change.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m10-genai-more~ta1_1","q":"<p>Trace an agent answering &quot;Which city had the biggest drop in orders last week?&quot; List the steps it should take, and two guardrails.</p>\n","a":"<p>Steps: (1) plan: compare orders per city for last week and the week before; (2) call the SQL tool with a query grouping orders by city and week; (3) compute the change per city from the result; (4) check the result (for example, that the totals match a known dashboard figure); (5) answer with the city, the size of the drop and the query used. Guardrails: read-only database credentials with a row limit and a timeout; a maximum number of tool calls per question; and logging each step so a human can audit it.</p>\n"}]},"m10-cv":{"id":"m10-cv","slug":"computer-vision-and-self-check","m":10,"title":"Computer vision and self-check","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"Medium-High","prereq":[],"tags":["computer-vision","deep-learning"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Computer vision essentials for data science roles: image classification with transfer learning, augmentation, detection metrics (IoU, mAP) and OCR for Indian document workflows; plus the Module 10 self-check.","keyTerms":["transfer learning","data augmentation","object detection","YOLO","IoU","mAP","OCR","KYC documents"],"interviewQs":["How do you build an image classifier with limited data?","What are IoU and mAP?","How would you extract fields from Aadhaar or PAN cards?"],"mistakes":["Training a CNN from scratch on a tiny dataset","Evaluating detection with accuracy","Handling identity documents without privacy controls"],"practice":"Fine-tune a pre-trained ResNet on a small two-class image dataset with augmentation.","related":["m10-dl-fundamentals","m7-core-concepts"],"points":"Image classification with transfer learning (ResNet/EfficientNet), augmentation, object detection (YOLO, Faster R-CNN) basics, IoU, mAP, OCR (Tesseract, PaddleOCR, cloud APIs), especially for Indian documents (Aadhaar/PAN KYC workflows).\n\n- Explain attention and why transformers replaced RNNs.\n- Draw a RAG architecture and name three failure modes and how you'd evaluate it.\n- Explain TF-IDF and when it beats embeddings."},"intuitive":"<p>Teaching a computer to see is like teaching a child who has already learned to recognise edges, shapes and textures. You do not start from zero: take a model that has already learned from millions of images and teach it your specific task (a few hundred photos of damaged parcels) by retraining only its last layers. To spot objects rather than label whole images, detection models draw boxes, and we score them by how much the box overlaps the real one (IoU).</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Only needed if the role involves images or documents, but transfer learning and OCR questions are common for KYC and insurance use cases in India.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Transfer learning plus augmentation; detection is judged by IoU and mAP; OCR pipelines need validation and privacy care.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Computer vision teaches models to understand images. Convolutional networks slide small filters over an image to detect edges, then textures, then parts, then objects, sharing weights so they need far fewer parameters than dense layers. Pooling shrinks the map. Tasks: classification, one label per image; detection, boxes plus labels; segmentation, a label per pixel. In practice I use transfer learning: take a model pretrained on a large dataset such as ImageNet, replace the final layer and fine-tune on my data, which works with small datasets. Data augmentation, like flips and crops, adds variety. Detection is scored by IoU and mean average precision. The catch: check that train and production images look alike.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Count a convolution's outputs and parameters","html":"<p>Input: a 32 × 32 colour image (3 channels). Layer: 16 filters of size 3 × 3, stride 1, padding 1.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Output size:</strong> (W − K + 2P) / S + 1 = (32 − 3 + 2) / 1 + 1 = <strong>32</strong>. With padding 1 the spatial size is kept: the output is 32 × 32 × 16.</li>\n<li><strong>Parameters per filter:</strong> 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 weights (the filter spans all 3 input channels) plus 1 bias = 28.</li>\n<li><strong>Parameters for the layer:</strong> 16 × 28 = <strong>448</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compare a dense layer</strong> from the same image (32 × 32 × 3 = 3,072 inputs) to just 16 units: 3,072 × 16 + 16 = <strong>49,168</strong> parameters, over 100 times more, with no sense of &quot;nearby pixels&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Why so few?</strong> Each filter is small and is <em>reused at every position</em> (weight sharing): an edge detector works the same at the top left and the bottom right.</li>\n<li><strong>Then pooling:</strong> a 2 × 2 max-pool halves width and height: 16 × 16 × 16.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Convolutions exploit two facts about images: nearby pixels matter together, and a pattern means the same thing wherever it appears. That is why CNNs need far fewer parameters than dense networks.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-10-4","text":"10.4 Computer vision (only if role requires)","level":2},{"id":"c-10-5","text":"10.5 Self-check","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-image-classification-with-limited-data","text":"Image classification with limited data","level":3},{"id":"h-object-detection","text":"Object detection","level":3},{"id":"h-ocr-and-indian-document-workflows","text":"OCR and Indian document workflows","level":3},{"id":"h-self-check-for-module-10","text":"Self-check for Module 10","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-10-4\"><span class=\"sec-num\">10.4</span> Computer vision (only if role requires)</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Image classification with transfer learning (ResNet/EfficientNet), augmentation, object detection (YOLO, Faster R-CNN) basics, IoU, mAP, OCR (Tesseract, PaddleOCR, cloud APIs), especially for Indian documents (Aadhaar/PAN KYC workflows).</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-10-5\"><span class=\"sec-num\">10.5</span> Self-check</h2>"},{"k":"cl","id":"m10-cv.c1","kind":"self","items":["Explain attention and why transformers replaced RNNs.","Draw a RAG architecture and name three failure modes and how you&#39;d evaluate it.","Explain TF-IDF and when it beats embeddings."]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-image-classification-with-limited-data\">Image classification with limited data</h3>\n<p>Use <strong>transfer learning</strong>: take a pre-trained backbone (ResNet, EfficientNet, a vision transformer), <strong>freeze</strong> most layers, replace the classification head, fine-tune. Add <strong>data augmentation</strong> (flips, crops, colour jitter, rotation) to stretch a small dataset. Normalise with the pre-trained model&#39;s mean and standard deviation, and validate on held-out images from different conditions.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> torch.nn <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> nn, torchvision\nmodel = torchvision.models.resnet18(weights=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DEFAULT&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> p <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> model.parameters(): p.requires_grad = <span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># freeze the backbone</span>\nmodel.fc = nn.Linear(model.fc.in_features, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)                    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># new trainable head for 2 classes</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-object-detection\">Object detection</h3>\n<p>Detectors (<strong>YOLO</strong>, Faster R-CNN, DETR) predict <strong>bounding boxes + class + confidence</strong>. <strong>IoU</strong> (intersection over union) = overlap area / union area of a predicted and a true box; a detection counts as correct when IoU exceeds a threshold (commonly 0.5). <strong>mAP</strong> averages precision over recall levels and classes (at one or several IoU thresholds). <strong>Non-maximum suppression</strong> removes duplicate boxes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-ocr-and-indian-document-workflows\">OCR and Indian document workflows</h3>\n<p>For <strong>KYC documents</strong> (Aadhaar, PAN, driving licence), a typical pipeline: image quality checks and de-skew, <strong>document classification</strong>, <strong>text detection and OCR</strong> (Tesseract, PaddleOCR, cloud OCR APIs), <strong>field extraction</strong> (regex, layout models, or an LLM with schema validation), <strong>validation rules</strong> (PAN format, date consistency, checksum where defined), a confidence threshold routing low-confidence cases to humans. Treat identity data as <strong>sensitive</strong>: mask, encrypt, restrict retention and access, and follow regulations such as India&#39;s DPDP Act principles and Aadhaar-specific rules.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-self-check-for-module-10\">Self-check for Module 10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain attention and why transformers replaced RNNs.</li>\n<li>Draw a RAG architecture and name three failure modes and how you would evaluate it.</li>\n<li>Explain TF-IDF and when it beats embeddings.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m10-cv~1","q":"You have 400 labelled photos of product defects. What is your plan?","a":"<p>Use <strong>transfer learning</strong> (pre-trained ResNet/EfficientNet), freeze most layers, fine-tune the head, and <strong>augment</strong> images to mimic real variations (lighting, angle, blur). Hold out a validation set that reflects production conditions (new days and cameras), handle class imbalance (weights), and choose metrics by defect cost (recall on critical defects). Inspect misclassified images for label noise, use Grad-CAM to check that the model looks at the defect and not at background artefacts, and collect more data where it fails.</p>\n","s":"Fine-tune a pre-trained CNN with augmentation, use stratified validation, and examine errors."},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout example\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Worked example</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p><strong>IoU and AP by hand.</strong> Ground-truth box: x 0–4, y 0–4 (area 16). Predicted box: x 2–6, y 0–4 (area 16). Overlap = x 2–4 × y 0–4 = 8. Union = 16 + 16 - 8 = 24. IoU = 8/24 = 0.33, below the common 0.5 threshold, so this prediction counts as a false positive. Shift the prediction to x 1–5 and the overlap is 12, union 20, IoU 0.6, a true positive. For average precision, rank predictions by confidence: TP, FP, TP with 2 real objects gives (precision, recall) points (1.0, 0.5), (0.5, 0.5), (0.67, 1.0). AP is the area under that precision-recall curve; mAP is the mean over classes (and sometimes over IoU thresholds).</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-cv~ca0_1","q":"Why do CNNs use fewer parameters than dense layers on images?","a":"<p>Small filters are shared across all positions (weight sharing) and connect locally.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-cv~ca0_2","q":"Only 500 labelled images. Approach?","a":"<p>Transfer learning from a pretrained model with augmentation; freeze early layers, fine-tune the last ones.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m10-cv~ca0_3","q":"Two boxes of area 16 overlap in area 8. IoU?","a":"<p>8 / (16 + 16 - 8) = 0.33.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m10-cv~ta1_1","q":"<p>A grey-scale 28 × 28 image goes through 8 filters of size 5 × 5, stride 1, no padding. What is the output shape, and how many parameters does the layer have?</p>\n","a":"<p>Output size = (28 − 5 + 0) / 1 + 1 = 24, so 24 × 24 × 8. Parameters: each filter has 5 × 5 × 1 = 25 weights plus 1 bias = 26; 8 filters give 208 parameters.</p>\n"}]}});